# SYSTEM(3)

## NAME

system - issue a shell command

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int system(const char *string);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*System* causes the *string* to be given to sh(1) as input as if the string had been typed as a
command at a terminal. The current process waits until the shell has completed, then returns
the exit status of the shell.

**Note:** a null *string* asks whether a command processor exists, which is C11 sec 7.22.4.8.
v7 had no such case.

## SEE ALSO

popen(3), exec(2), wait(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Exit status 127 indicates the shell couldn't be executed.

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/stdio/system.c

## BESM-6 NOTES

The status is wait(2)'s and is **only 15 bits wide here** - it comes back in an index register
\- so exit codes 128..255 arrive truncated. See wait(2).

**This routine is tested in both harnesses and they test opposite things.** Under b6sim,
/bin/sh is a host binary and not a BESM-6 *a.out*, so the child always fails to exec and
reports it with the 127 above; that is lib/test/spawn, and it is a b6sim-only test for exactly
that reason. Off the disk image, /bin/sh really does start, and lib/test/shellt is the other
half.
